## Who gets free prescriptions in England

You do not pay for NHS prescriptions if, when you get your medicine, you:

- are **60 or over**
- are **under 16**
- are **16, 17 or 18 and in full-time education**
- are **pregnant or have had a baby in the last 12 months** and have a valid **maternity exemption certificate (MatEx)**
- have a **medical exemption certificate (MedEx)**
- have a **war pension exemption certificate** (for your accepted disability)
- are an **NHS inpatient**
- have an **HC2 certificate** from the NHS Low Income Scheme
- get, or your partner gets, **income-related ESA** or **Pension Credit Guarantee Credit**
- get **Universal Credit** and meet the earnings rules below

### Universal Credit earnings rules

You get free prescriptions if, in your **last assessment period**, your take-home pay (or joint take-home pay if you claim as a couple) was:

- **£435 or less**, or
- **£935 or less** if your Universal Credit includes an amount for a **child** or for **limited capability for work**

Your earnings are checked each month, so check your latest statement.

{% callout type="info" title="Income Support and JSA" %}
From **15 April 2026**, Income Support and income-based Jobseeker's Allowance no longer give free prescriptions. **Income-related ESA** and **Pension Credit Guarantee Credit** still do.
{% /callout %}

## The medical exemption list

You can get a **MedEx certificate** if you have:

- cancer, and are having treatment for cancer or its effects, or the effects of treatment
- a permanent fistula, such as a stoma, that needs a dressing or appliance
- Addison's disease or another form of hypoadrenalism
- diabetes insipidus or another form of hypopituitarism
- **diabetes** treated with medicine (not diet alone)
- hypoparathyroidism
- myasthenia gravis
- an **underactive thyroid** needing thyroid hormone
- **epilepsy** needing continuous medicine
- a continuing **physical disability** that means you cannot go out without help from another person

Ask your GP surgery for **form FP92A**. The certificate usually lasts 5 years.

## If you have to pay

A **prescription prepayment certificate (PPC)** covers all your prescriptions for a fixed price. It saves money if you need more than a few items:

- a **3 month PPC** saves money if you need **4 or more items** in 3 months
- a **12 month PPC** saves money if you need **12 or more items** in a year, and can be paid in **10 monthly instalments**
- an **HRT PPC** covers certain hormone replacement therapy medicines for 12 months

You can buy a PPC online from the NHS Business Services Authority, or at many pharmacies.

## In Wales

Prescriptions are **free for everyone** registered with a GP in Wales, including when you collect them from a pharmacy in Wales.
